%I A302109 #80 Oct 18 2026 09:47:02
%S A302109 1,3,8,24,840,9240,212520,9988440,589317960,48913390680,5233732802760,
%T A302109 874033378060920
%N A302109 Smallest integer N such that there are exactly n cyclic groups C_2 in the multiplicative group of integers modulo N when decomposed as a product of cyclic groups C_{k_1} x C_{k_2} x ... x C_{k_m}, and k_i divides k_j for i < j.
%C A302109 a(n) exists for all n: by Dirichlet's theorem on arithmetic progressions, there exists primes of the form 2*c_1 + 1, 2*c_2 + 1, ..., 2*c_(n+1) + 1 where c_i are pairwise coprime odd numbers, then the multiplicative group of integers modulo (2*c_1 + 1)(2*c_2 + 1)*...*(2*c_(n+1) + 1) is isomorphic to (C_2)^n x C_(2*c_1*c_2*...*c_(n+1)).
%C A302109 Conjecture: (a) a(j) is divisible by a(i) if i < j; (b) a(n)/4 is squarefree for all n for n >= 2. [Corrected by _Alper Ferudun_ and _Jianing Song_, Oct 18 2026]
%C A302109 From _Jianing Song_, Oct 17 2026: (Start)
%C A302109 For a finite group G = C_{m_1} X ... X C_{m_k} and a prime power q, let r_q(G) be the number of m_i divisible by q. Then a(n) is the least N such that r_2((Z/NZ)*) - max{r_4((Z/NZ)*),r_p((Z/NZ)*) : p odd prime} = n.
%C A302109 Conjecture (b) above is obvious according to this description, while conjecture (a) is false: The smallest multiple N of a(11) such that (Z/NZ)* contains 12 copies of C_2 is N = 179*a(11) = 156451974672904680, but a(12) is at most 120025194365695080. (End)
%H A302109 Jianing Song, <a href="/A302109/a302109_4.txt">Conjectured a(0)-a(61) and their factorizations</a>.
%H A302109 Jianing Song, <a href="/A302109/a302109_6.txt">Proof that a(11) = 874033378060920</a>.
%e A302109 The multiplicative group of integers modulo 212520 is isomorphic to (C_2)^6 x C_660 and 212520 is the smallest number N such that the multiplicative group of integers modulo N contains six C_2 as the product of cyclic groups, so a(6) = 212520.
%e A302109 a(17) is perhaps N = 2^3 * 3 * 5 * 7 * 11 * 17 * 19 * 23 * 47 * 59 * 71 * 83 * 107 * 167 * 179 * 227 * 239 * 263, since the multiplicative group of integers modulo N is isomorphic to (C_2)^17 x C_(4*3*5*7) x C_(16*9*5*7*11*17*23*29*41*53*83*89*113*131). [Corrected by _Alper Ferudun_ and _Jianing Song_, Oct 18 2026]
